Here's one from the repo. `internal/topology/testdata/messagefirst_system_graph.json` sets it in three places. This is the trimmed version, showing only the fields `buildPubSubs` reads:

```json
{
  "apiVersion": "topology.intropy.io/v1",
  "kind": "SystemTopology",
  "system": "order-flow",
  "components": [
    {
      "name": "order-extractor",
      "kind": "extractor",
      "publishes":  [{ "pubsub": "pubsub", "topic": "orders" }]
    },
    {
      "name": "order-loader",
      "kind": "loader",
      "subscribes": [{ "pubsub": "pubsub", "topic": "orders" }]
    }
  ],
  "topics": [
    {
      "pubsub": "pubsub",
      "topic": "orders",
      "contract": "Contracts.Order",
      "publishers":  ["order-extractor"],
      "subscribers": ["order-loader"]
    }
  ]
}
```

Every reference uses the same name, `"pubsub"`. So `buildPubSubs` produces one entry:

```
ManifestPubSub{Name: "pubsub", Topics: ["orders"], AppIDs: [order-extractor, order-loader]}
```

Because there's exactly one, the template gets `pubsubName: "pubsub"`. The local render also sets `pubsub: "rabbitmq"` as the broker type.

If you want a different component name, say `orders-bus`, you'd change every `"pubsub"` value above. If you change only some of them, the topology references two pubsubs (`pubsub` and `orders-bus`), and `pubsubName` isn't set at all.

The full fixture also has a `messagegroups[].messages[].channel.pubsub` field. `buildPubSubs` doesn't read it; it only looks at `topics[]` and the components' `publishes` and `subscribes`. So a name that appears only in a message channel never becomes a Dapr component.

